A computable element is the decreasing union of the principal elements named by a primitive-recursive sequence of indices.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`ComputableWitness.tFun`).** From a sequence of presentation indices, form a new sequence by taking the first index initially and then repeatedly choosing an index for the intersection with the next neighbourhood.

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.repred_exists_primrec_range`).** Every nonempty recursively enumerable predicate on the natural numbers is the range of a primitive-recursive function.

*Proof.* Enumerate witnesses to the predicate and return a fixed known witness whenever the current search certificate fails. ∎

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.tFun_zero`).** The initial value of the thinning sequence is the initial value of the original sequence.

*Proof.* This is the initial clause of the recursion. ∎

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.tFun_succ`).** Each successor value of the thinning sequence is the chosen intersection index of its preceding value and the next original value.

*Proof.* This is the successor clause of the recursion. ∎

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.primrec_tFun`).** Thinning a primitive-recursive sequence along a computable presentation produces a primitive-recursive sequence.

*Proof.* Primitive recursion applies because the presentation's intersection-index operation and the original sequence are primitive recursive. ∎

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.computableElement_eq_decreasing_iUnion_principal`).** Every computable element is generated by a primitive-recursive decreasing sequence of principal elements: a neighbourhood belongs to the element exactly when it belongs to one of those principals.

*Proof.* Enumerate the indices of neighbourhoods containing the element, starting with a master index, and thin this enumeration by successive intersections. Every original neighbourhood occurs above the corresponding thinned neighbourhood, while every thinned neighbourhood still contains the element. ∎

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.repred_range_primrec`).** The range of a primitive-recursive function is recursively enumerable.

*Proof.* A number belongs to the range exactly when some coded pair has first component mapped to its second component. ∎

**Theorem (`ComputableWitness.repred₂_exists_primrec_enum`).** Every inhabited recursively enumerable binary relation has a primitive-recursive enumeration by two coordinate functions.

*Proof.* Enumerate the paired relation by one primitive-recursive function and take the two projections of each value. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_upSet_inter`).** The intersection of the upper sets generated by two neighbourhoods is a power-domain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* It is the upper set of their intersection when that intersection is a neighbourhood, and is empty otherwise; both forms are power-domain neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.upSetUnion_cons`).** The union of upper sets indexed by a list with head \(Y\) is the union of the upper set of \(Y\) and the union indexed by the tail.

*Proof.* Split membership in the list according to whether the chosen entry is the head or lies in the tail. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.upSetUnion_nil`).** The union of upper sets indexed by the empty list is empty.

*Proof.* The empty list has no indexing member. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_upSet_inter_biUnion`).** The intersection of one principal upper set with any finite union of principal upper sets is a power-domain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Induct on the list and distribute intersection over union, using closure under binary union. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_biUnion_inter`).** The intersection of a finite union of principal upper sets with any power-domain neighbourhood is a power-domain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Induct on the first finite list and use the preceding principal-intersection result. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_inter`).** Power-domain neighbourhoods are closed under intersection.

*Proof.* Represent the first neighbourhood as a finite union of principal upper sets and apply the preceding theorem. ∎
